# Chapter 1 · Growth, Development & Immunization (owner, 12 Nov 2026: levels and clean wording as in the Obstetrics book).
# Card: (old index, section, question, answer, level). Facts, numbers and doses are unchanged; shorthand is written out in full.
# Dropped: growth 6 (the same card is ftt 26, in its own section).
CARDS = {
'growth': [
(0, 'Definitions', 'What is growth?', 'Growth is an increase in body size, from an increase in cell number and cell size. It is measured by weight, length or height, and head circumference.', 1),
(1, 'Definitions', 'What is the neonatal period?', 'From birth to 28 days of life.', 1),
(2, 'Phases', 'When is postnatal growth fastest?', 'In infancy (the first year of life). There is a second peak at the pubertal growth spurt.', 1),
(3, 'Phases', 'What is the normal height velocity in mid-childhood?', '5–7 cm per year. A velocity below 4 cm per year before puberty is abnormal.', 2),
(4, 'Factors', 'What is the most important postnatal factor that affects growth?', 'Nutrition: protein–energy malnutrition and poor complementary feeding.', 1),
(5, 'Factors', 'Which hormone mainly drives fetal growth?', 'Insulin, together with the insulin-like growth factors (IGFs). Growth hormone has little effect before birth.', 2),
(7, 'Factors', 'Which hormone fuses the epiphyses and stops growth?', 'Oestrogen, in both boys and girls.', 2),
(8, 'Measurement', 'Up to what age is length measured lying down?', 'Under 2 years of age, supine on a length board.', 1),
(9, 'Measurement', 'For how long is the growth chart corrected for prematurity?', 'Until 2 years of age; the measurements are plotted at the corrected age.', 1),
(10, 'Weight', 'What is the average birth weight of a term baby?', '3–3.5 kg. Low birth weight is a birth weight below 2.5 kg.', 1),
(11, 'Weight', 'How much weight does a newborn lose after birth, and when is it regained?', 'Up to 10% of the birth weight in the first 3–4 days (physiological weight loss). The birth weight is regained by 10–14 days.', 1),
(12, 'Weight', 'When does the birth weight double, triple and quadruple?', 'It doubles at 4–5 months, triples at 1 year and quadruples at 2 years.', 1),
(13, 'Weight', 'What is the monthly weight gain in the first 4 months?', 'About ¾ kg per month (25–30 g per day).', 1),
(14, 'Weight', 'What is the weight formula for children aged 1–6 years?', 'Weight (kg) = (age in years × 2) + 8.', 1),
(15, 'Weight', 'What is the weight formula for infants aged 3–12 months?', 'Weight (kg) = (age in months + 9) ÷ 2.', 1),
(16, 'Length', 'What is the length at birth and at 1 year?', '50 cm at birth and 75 cm at 1 year: an increase of 25 cm in the first year.', 1),
(17, 'Length', 'At what age is the birth length doubled?', 'At 4 years (about 100 cm).', 1),
(18, 'Length', 'What is the height formula for children aged 2–12 years?', 'Height (cm) = (age in years × 5) + 80.', 1),
(19, 'Length', 'How is the mid-parental height of a boy calculated?', '(Father\'s height + mother\'s height) ÷ 2 + 6.5 cm. The expected range is ± 10 cm.', 2),
(20, 'Proportions', 'What is the upper-to-lower segment ratio at birth, and when does it reach 1?', 'It is 1.7 at birth and reaches 1.0 at 7–10 years.', 2),
(21, 'Proportions', 'What are the causes of short stature with a high upper-to-lower segment ratio (short limbs)?', 'Achondroplasia, rickets and congenital hypothyroidism.', 2),
(22, 'Proportions', 'What does an arm span that exceeds the height by more than 5 cm suggest?', 'Marfan syndrome.', 2),
(23, 'Head', 'What is the head circumference at birth and at 1 year?', '35 cm at birth and 47 cm at 1 year: an increase of 12 cm in the first year.', 1),
(24, 'Head', 'How fast does the head circumference grow in the first year?', '2 cm per month from 0 to 3 months, 1 cm per month from 3 to 6 months and 0.5 cm per month from 6 to 12 months.', 2),
(25, 'Head', 'When are the head and chest circumferences equal?', 'At about 1 year. After that, the chest circumference is larger.', 1),
(26, 'Head', 'How is microcephaly defined?', 'A head circumference below −2 standard deviations (the 3rd centile) for age and sex.', 1),
(27, 'Head', 'What are the perinatal and postnatal causes of microcephaly?', 'Perinatal: birth asphyxia (hypoxic–ischaemic encephalopathy). Postnatal: meningitis and encephalitis.', 2),
(28, 'Head', 'Name three bony (skull) causes of macrocephaly.', 'Rickets, chronic haemolytic anaemia (thalassaemia) and achondroplasia.', 2),
(29, 'Head', 'What is the commonest cause of macrocephaly in a normal child?', 'Familial (benign) macrocephaly.', 2),
(30, 'Fontanelles', 'When does the anterior fontanelle close?', 'At 12–18 months.', 1),
(31, 'Fontanelles', 'When does the posterior fontanelle close?', 'By 2 months.', 1),
(32, 'Fontanelles', 'What are the causes of a large or late-closing anterior fontanelle?', 'Hypothyroidism, rickets, Down syndrome, achondroplasia, osteogenesis imperfecta and raised intracranial pressure.', 2),
(33, 'Fontanelles', 'What does a depressed (sunken) fontanelle indicate?', 'Dehydration.', 1),
(34, 'Teeth', 'When does the first tooth erupt, and which tooth is it?', 'At about 6 months; it is a lower central incisor.', 1),
(35, 'Teeth', 'How is delayed teething defined?', 'No tooth by 13 months of age.', 1),
(36, 'Teeth', 'Which is the first permanent tooth, and when does it erupt?', 'The first molar, at 6 years.', 1),
(37, 'Bone age', 'Which X-ray is used to assess bone age after 1 year of age?', 'An X-ray of the left hand and wrist, read with the Greulich–Pyle atlas.', 1),
(38, 'Bone age', 'Which disorder delays the bone age the most?', 'Hypothyroidism.', 2),
(39, 'Bone age', 'What are the causes of an advanced bone age?', 'Precocious puberty, congenital adrenal hyperplasia, hyperthyroidism and obesity.', 2),
(40, 'MUAC', 'What is the mid-upper arm circumference (MUAC) cut-off for severe acute malnutrition in children aged 6–59 months?', 'A MUAC below 11.5 cm (the red zone).', 1),
(41, 'Charts', 'What is the normal range on a WHO growth chart?', 'Between −2 and +2 standard deviations (about the 3rd to 97th centile), with the child following his or her own channel.', 2),
(42, 'Charts', 'Which growth indicator shows chronic malnutrition?', 'Length- or height-for-age: a low value is stunting.', 2),
(43, 'Charts', 'Which growth indicator shows acute malnutrition?', 'Weight-for-length or weight-for-height: a low value is wasting.', 2),
(44, 'Charts', 'How is growth faltering recognised on a growth chart?', 'The weight crosses two major centile lines downwards, or the weight curve becomes flat.', 2),
(45, 'Charts', 'A child is short but overweight for his height. Which group of causes should be considered?', 'An endocrine cause: hypothyroidism, growth hormone deficiency or Cushing syndrome.', 3),
],
'development': [
(0, 'Principles', 'Name the four fields of development.', 'Gross motor; fine motor and vision; hearing, speech and language; and social, emotional and behavioural development.', 1),
(1, 'Principles', 'In which direction does motor development proceed?', 'From head to feet (head control, then sitting, standing and walking), and from reflex to voluntary movement.', 1),
(2, 'Principles', 'What is the difference between the median age and the limit age of a milestone?', 'The median age is the age at which half of children have the skill. The limit age is about 2 standard deviations later, by which almost all children have it.', 2),
(3, 'Principles', 'Until what age is the corrected age used for preterm infants?', 'Until 2 years of age.', 1),
(4, 'Principles', 'What are the features of a bottom shuffler?', 'The child often skips crawling and walks late (18–24 months). It is often familial and the tone is normal. It is a normal variant.', 2),
(5, 'Reflexes', 'When does the Moro reflex disappear?', 'At 4–6 months.', 1),
(6, 'Reflexes', 'What are the causes of a Moro reflex that is absent on both sides in a newborn?', 'Depression of the central nervous system: hypoxic–ischaemic encephalopathy, kernicterus, intracranial bleeding, sepsis or sedation.', 2),
(7, 'Reflexes', 'What are the causes of a Moro reflex that is absent on one side?', 'Erb\'s palsy, a fractured clavicle or humerus, or a dislocated shoulder.', 2),
(8, 'Reflexes', 'When does the asymmetrical tonic neck reflex (ATNR) disappear?', 'By 6 months. An obligatory ATNR is abnormal at any age.', 2),
(9, 'Reflexes', 'When does the palmar grasp reflex disappear?', 'At 3–4 months.', 1),
(10, 'Reflexes', 'Which reaction appears at 9 months and persists for life?', 'The parachute reaction. It is a protective reaction, not a primitive reflex.', 2),
(11, 'Reflexes', 'Is an extensor plantar response normal at 7 months?', 'Yes. It is normal until the child walks (12–18 months).', 2),
(12, 'Reflexes', 'What does persistence of primitive reflexes after 6 months suggest?', 'Cerebral palsy.', 2),
(13, 'First year', 'At what age does the social smile appear?', 'At 6 weeks.', 1),
(14, 'First year', 'At what age is head control achieved?', 'At 3 months. Head lag after 4 months is abnormal.', 1),
(15, 'First year', 'At what age does an infant transfer objects from hand to hand?', 'At 6 months.', 1),
(16, 'First year', 'At what age does an infant sit without support?', 'At 7–8 months; the limit age is 9 months.', 1),
(17, 'First year', 'At what age does an infant crawl and pull to stand?', 'At 9 months.', 1),
(18, 'First year', 'At what age does stranger anxiety appear?', 'At 9 months.', 1),
(19, 'First year', 'At what age does an infant wave bye-bye and say "mama" and "dada"?', 'At about 10 months.', 1),
(20, 'First year', 'At what age is the mature pincer grip present?', 'At 12 months. An immature pincer grip is seen at 9 months.', 1),
(21, 'First year', 'What are the median age and the limit age for walking alone?', 'The median age is 12–13 months; the limit age is 18 months.', 2),
(22, 'First year', 'At what age is the distraction hearing test done?', 'At 7–9 months.', 1),
(23, 'After 1 year', 'At what age does a child build a tower of 2 cubes?', 'At 15 months.', 1),
(24, 'After 1 year', 'What are the speech and social skills of an 18-month-old child?', 'The child uses 6–20 words, points to parts of the body and feeds with a spoon.', 1),
(25, 'After 1 year', 'At what age does a child speak in phrases of 2–3 words?', 'At 2 years.', 1),
(26, 'After 1 year', 'At what age does a child go up and down stairs with two feet on each step?', 'At 2 years.', 1),
(27, 'After 1 year', 'At what age does a child ride a tricycle?', 'At 3 years.', 1),
(28, 'After 1 year', 'At what age does a child give his or her full name, age and sex?', 'At 3 years.', 1),
(29, 'After 1 year', 'At what age does a child hop on one foot?', 'At 4 years.', 1),
(30, 'After 1 year', 'At what age does a child skip?', 'At 5 years.', 1),
(31, 'Shapes', 'In what order, and at what ages, does a child copy shapes?', 'A line at 2 years, a circle at 3 years, a cross at 4 years, a square at 4½ years, a triangle at 5 years and a diamond at 6 years.', 2),
(32, 'Delay', 'What is global developmental delay?', 'Delay in two or more fields of development.', 1),
(33, 'Delay', 'Why is developmental regression always serious?', 'Loss of skills already gained suggests a neurodegenerative or metabolic disease, autism or uncontrolled epilepsy.', 3),
(34, 'Delay', 'What is the limit age for the first words?', '18 months. No two-word phrases at 2 years is also abnormal.', 2),
(35, 'Delay', 'What does hand preference before 12 months of age suggest?', 'Weakness of the other side (hemiplegic cerebral palsy).', 3),
(36, 'Delay', 'What is the first investigation in a child with isolated speech delay?', 'A hearing test.', 2),
(37, 'Delay', 'What are the perinatal causes of global developmental delay?', 'Birth asphyxia (hypoxic–ischaemic encephalopathy), prematurity (intraventricular haemorrhage, periventricular leukomalacia), kernicterus and hypoglycaemia.', 2),
(38, 'Delay', 'A boy is late to walk. Which blood test is essential?', 'Creatine kinase (CK), to look for Duchenne muscular dystrophy.', 3),
(39, 'Delay', 'What are the social features of autism?', 'Poor eye contact, no pointing, no pretend play, repetitive behaviour, and delayed speech or loss of speech.', 2),
],
'ftt': [
(0, 'FTT', 'Is failure to thrive a diagnosis?', 'No. It is a sign of inadequate weight gain, and its cause must be found.', 1),
(1, 'FTT', 'How is failure to thrive defined?', 'A weight-for-age below −2 standard deviations on repeated measurements, a weight that crosses two major centile lines downwards, or a weight-for-length below −2 standard deviations.', 2),
(2, 'FTT', 'What is the commonest cause of failure to thrive?', 'Inadequate intake (malnutrition).', 1),
(3, 'FTT', 'Is breastfeeding a cause of failure to thrive?', 'No. This is the classic "all EXCEPT" answer.', 2),
(4, 'FTT', 'Which feeding errors cause failure to thrive in infants?', 'Poor breastfeeding technique and over-diluted formula; also late or poor complementary feeding.', 2),
(5, 'FTT', 'Name causes of failure to thrive due to increased needs.', 'Congenital heart disease with heart failure, tuberculosis, HIV infection, kala-azar, chronic lung disease and hyperthyroidism.', 2),
(6, 'FTT', 'Which renal disease causes failure to thrive with polyuria and rickets?', 'Renal tubular acidosis.', 2),
(7, 'FTT', 'What are the signs of non-organic failure to thrive?', 'An occipital bald patch, poor hygiene, avoidance of eye contact, delayed milestones, and rapid weight gain when the child is fed adequately.', 2),
(8, 'FTT', 'An underweight infant gains weight quickly in hospital. What is the diagnosis?', 'Non-organic (psychosocial) failure to thrive.', 3),
(9, 'FTT', 'What provides the basis of the diagnosis in failure to thrive?', 'The history and physical examination.', 1),
(10, 'FTT', 'What are the first-line investigations in failure to thrive?', 'Complete blood count, urine analysis and culture, stool for parasites and reducing substances, electrolytes, liver function tests and thyroid function tests.', 2),
(11, 'FTT', 'Which test is used to screen for coeliac disease?', 'Anti-tissue transglutaminase IgA (tTG IgA).', 1),
(12, 'FTT', 'How many calories are needed for catch-up growth in failure to thrive?', 'About 150 kcal/kg/day, calculated on the expected weight. A normal infant needs 100–120 kcal/kg/day.', 2),
(13, 'FTT', 'What are the indications to admit a child with failure to thrive?', 'Complicated severe acute malnutrition, suspected abuse or neglect, failure of outpatient care, or the need to observe feeding.', 2),
(14, 'FTT', 'According to IMCI, in which children should feeding be assessed?', 'In children under 2 years, and in any child with anaemia or moderate acute malnutrition.', 2),
(15, 'Short stature', 'How is short stature defined?', 'A height below −2 standard deviations (the 3rd centile) for age and sex.', 1),
(16, 'Short stature', 'What height velocity is abnormal before puberty?', 'A height velocity below 4 cm per year.', 1),
(17, 'Short stature', 'What are the commonest causes of short stature?', 'Normal variants: familial short stature and constitutional delay of growth.', 1),
(18, 'Short stature', 'In familial short stature, what are the bone age and the timing of puberty?', 'The bone age equals the actual age, and puberty starts at a normal age.', 2),
(19, 'Short stature', 'In constitutional delay of growth, what are the bone age, puberty and final height?', 'The bone age is delayed, puberty is delayed, and the final height is normal.', 2),
(20, 'Short stature', 'Does acute renal failure cause short stature?', 'No. Only chronic kidney disease does.', 2),
(21, 'Short stature', 'Name causes of disproportionate short stature with short limbs.', 'Achondroplasia, rickets and osteogenesis imperfecta.', 2),
(22, 'Short stature', 'What are the causes of short stature with a short trunk?', 'The mucopolysaccharidoses (such as Morquio syndrome) and disease of the spine.', 2),
(23, 'Short stature', 'What are the causes of a child who is short and overweight for height?', 'Hypothyroidism, growth hormone deficiency, and Cushing syndrome or steroid treatment.', 2),
(24, 'Short stature', 'What are the causes of a child who is short and thin?', 'Malnutrition, coeliac disease, chronic kidney disease, renal tubular acidosis, congenital heart disease, cystic fibrosis and emotional deprivation.', 2),
(25, 'Short stature', 'An overweight child has failed to grow and has a delayed bone age. What is the likely cause?', 'Hypothyroidism.', 3),
(26, 'GH deficiency', 'What is the birth size in congenital growth hormone deficiency?', 'Normal. Growth slows after infancy, because growth hormone has little effect before birth.', 2),
(27, 'GH deficiency', 'What are the neonatal clues to hypopituitarism?', 'Micropenis, hypoglycaemia and prolonged jaundice.', 2),
(28, 'GH deficiency', 'What does a child with growth hormone deficiency look like?', 'Proportionate short stature, chubby build, an immature doll-like face, a high-pitched voice and a delayed bone age.', 2),
(29, 'GH deficiency', 'Which tumour causes growth hormone deficiency with loss of the visual fields?', 'Craniopharyngioma.', 2),
(30, 'GH deficiency', 'How is growth hormone deficiency confirmed?', 'A low IGF-1 and two growth hormone stimulation tests with a low peak (below 10 ng/mL), followed by an MRI of the pituitary.', 3),
(31, 'GH deficiency', 'A short child has a high growth hormone level and a low IGF-1. What is the diagnosis?', 'Laron syndrome (growth hormone resistance).', 3),
(32, 'Assessment', 'What is the first investigation in a short child?', 'Bone age: an X-ray of the left hand and wrist.', 1),
(33, 'Assessment', 'Which test must be done in every short girl without an obvious cause?', 'A karyotype, to exclude Turner syndrome.', 2),
(34, 'Assessment', 'Why is thyroid function checked before growth hormone testing?', 'Hypothyroidism lowers growth hormone secretion and gives a falsely low result.', 3),
(35, 'Treatment', 'What are the indications for growth hormone therapy?', 'Growth hormone deficiency, Turner syndrome, chronic kidney disease, Prader–Willi syndrome, and children born small for gestational age without catch-up growth.', 2),
(36, 'Treatment', 'How is constitutional delay of growth treated?', 'With reassurance. A short course of sex steroid is given if puberty is very late or the child is distressed.', 2),
],
'immunization': [
(0, 'Immunity', 'Give examples of natural passive immunity.', 'Maternal IgG that crosses the placenta, and IgA in breast milk.', 1),
(1, 'Immunity', 'Give examples of acquired passive immunity.', 'Immunoglobulins and antitoxins: hepatitis B immunoglobulin, tetanus immunoglobulin and rabies immunoglobulin.', 1),
(2, 'Types', 'Name the live attenuated vaccines of the EPI.', 'BCG, oral polio vaccine, measles (MR) vaccine and rotavirus vaccine (and yellow fever vaccine).', 1),
(3, 'Types', 'Is the pertussis vaccine live?', 'No. The whole-cell pertussis vaccine is killed. This is the classic "all are live EXCEPT" answer.', 2),
(4, 'Types', 'Which vaccines are toxoids?', 'Diphtheria and tetanus vaccines.', 1),
(5, 'Types', 'What type of vaccine is the hepatitis B vaccine?', 'A recombinant subunit vaccine (hepatitis B surface antigen, HBsAg).', 1),
(6, 'Types', 'Why are the Hib and pneumococcal conjugate vaccines joined to a protein?', 'Conjugation makes them work in children under 2 years and gives immune memory, through a T-cell response.', 3),
(7, 'Schedule', 'Which vaccines are given at birth in the EPI?', 'BCG and the birth dose of oral polio vaccine (OPV 0).', 1),
(8, 'Schedule', 'Which vaccines are given at 6 and 10 weeks?', 'Oral polio vaccine, pentavalent vaccine, pneumococcal conjugate vaccine and rotavirus vaccine.', 1),
(9, 'Schedule', 'Which vaccines are given at 14 weeks?', 'The third doses of oral polio, pentavalent and pneumococcal conjugate vaccines, and inactivated polio vaccine (IPV). Rotavirus vaccine is not given.', 2),
(10, 'Schedule', 'Which vaccines are given at 9 months?', 'The first dose of measles (MR) vaccine and meningococcal A vaccine; yellow fever vaccine in states at risk.', 1),
(11, 'Schedule', 'Which vaccine is given at 18 months?', 'The second dose of measles (MR) vaccine.', 1),
(12, 'Schedule', 'What does the pentavalent vaccine contain?', 'Diphtheria, tetanus, pertussis, hepatitis B and Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib).', 1),
(13, 'Schedule', 'What vitamin A doses are given with the EPI?', '100 000 IU at 6 months, then 200 000 IU every 6 months until 5 years of age.', 2),
(14, 'Schedule', 'How is neonatal tetanus prevented?', 'By tetanus toxoid (Td) vaccination of the mother, clean delivery and clean cord care.', 2),
(15, 'Routes', 'What are the dose and route of BCG?', '0.05 ml intradermally in the left upper arm.', 1),
(16, 'Routes', 'What are the dose and route of the measles (MR) vaccine?', '0.5 ml subcutaneously in the right upper arm.', 1),
(17, 'Routes', 'Where are the pentavalent and pneumococcal conjugate vaccines given in infants?', 'Intramuscularly in the anterolateral thigh: pentavalent in the left thigh and pneumococcal conjugate vaccine in the right thigh.', 2),
(18, 'Reactions', 'What is the normal course after BCG vaccination?', 'A papule appears at 2–6 weeks, becomes a small ulcer, and heals as a scar by 3 months.', 1),
(19, 'Reactions', 'What are the complications of BCG?', 'Axillary lymphadenitis and abscess; disseminated BCG infection in immunodeficiency.', 2),
(20, 'Reactions', 'Which vaccine causes febrile seizures and persistent crying?', 'The pertussis component (pentavalent vaccine).', 2),
(21, 'Reactions', 'What is the rare serious complication of rotavirus vaccine?', 'Intussusception.', 1),
(22, 'Reactions', 'What is the normal reaction 7–12 days after measles vaccination?', 'Fever and a mild rash.', 2),
(23, 'Reactions', 'Which polio vaccine gives herd immunity and gut immunity?', 'Oral polio vaccine, because the vaccine virus is shed in the stool.', 2),
(24, 'Reactions', 'What is the rare complication of oral polio vaccine?', 'Vaccine-associated paralytic polio.', 1),
(25, 'Reactions', 'How is anaphylaxis after a vaccine treated?', 'Adrenaline 1:1000, 0.01 mg/kg intramuscularly, to a maximum of 0.5 ml.', 2),
(26, 'Contraindications', 'What is a contraindication to all vaccines?', 'Anaphylaxis to a previous dose or to a component of the vaccine.', 1),
(27, 'Contraindications', 'What are the contraindications to live vaccines?', 'Severe immunodeficiency, chemotherapy, high-dose steroids and pregnancy.', 2),
(28, 'Contraindications', 'What is the absolute contraindication to pertussis vaccine?', 'Encephalopathy within 7 days of a previous dose, or a progressive neurological disease.', 2),
(29, 'Contraindications', 'What is the contraindication to BCG?', 'Symptomatic HIV infection or a known immunodeficiency.', 2),
(30, 'Contraindications', 'What are the contraindications to rotavirus vaccine?', 'Previous intussusception and severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID).', 2),
(31, 'Contraindications', 'Which of these is a contraindication to measles vaccine: a febrile child, a diabetic mother, or pregnancy?', 'Pregnancy, because the measles vaccine is live.', 3),
(32, 'Contraindications', 'Are antibiotic treatment or mild diarrhoea contraindications to vaccination?', 'No. The child should be vaccinated.', 2),
(33, 'Contraindications', 'Which polio vaccine is given to a household contact of an immunodeficient child?', 'Inactivated polio vaccine (IPV) instead of oral polio vaccine.', 3),
(34, 'Special', 'How is a preterm baby vaccinated?', 'According to the chronological age, with full doses.', 2),
(35, 'Special', 'What is done when vaccine doses are missed?', 'The series is never restarted; it is continued with intervals of 4 weeks.', 2),
(36, 'Special', 'How is the newborn of an HBsAg-positive mother managed?', 'Hepatitis B immunoglobulin and hepatitis B vaccine within 12 hours of birth, then completion of the vaccine series. Breastfeeding continues.', 3),
(37, 'Special', 'An unvaccinated child over 6 months old is a contact of measles. What should be given?', 'Measles vaccine within 72 hours of the contact.', 3),
(38, 'Special', 'An unvaccinated child has a dirty wound. What should be given?', 'Td vaccine and tetanus immunoglobulin.', 3),
(39, 'Special', 'Which extra vaccines are given in sickle cell disease?', 'Pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccine (PPV23) after 2 years, meningococcal vaccine and yearly influenza vaccine, together with penicillin prophylaxis.', 2),
(40, 'Special', 'What is the rabies post-exposure schedule?', 'Rabies vaccine intramuscularly on days 0, 3, 7 and 14, with rabies immunoglobulin for category III exposure.', 2),
(41, 'Special', 'Which vaccines must never be frozen?', 'Pentavalent, hepatitis B and pneumococcal conjugate vaccines. The shake test detects a vaccine that has been frozen.', 2),
],
}

